A host builds a turn's boundary context only when a handler reads it.

At each turn's end and before a run settles, pi builds the boundary
context, the session's branch copied into a session of its own,
projected and converted for the model, before the first handler and
again after every one, whether a handler read it or returned anything.
On a long session each build holds the main thread tens of milliseconds,
in a process every conversation of a host shares, and keys typed into
any of them wait behind it. The harness's turn_end handlers only
observe, so all of it was for nothing. Now it is built when read, and
afresh after each handler, which may have changed what it holds. Entries
a handler returns are still built at once, to check them.

Apply only to the host's private pi build; ordinary interactive pi stays
unchanged.

